-- SessionRuntimeEvent(type='text_delta', data={'content': 'Verification passed.
Final summary:\n\n## Change\n\n**`src/components/AsyncPanel.tsx`**\n- Added an 
`attempt` counter that the effect now depends on, and a `retryingRef` guard so a
retry can only be scheduled once (no overlapping timers; the existing cleanup 
still clears the timer and a `cancelled` flag also guards the callback).\n- On 
retry: `setState(\'loading\')` renders `Checking service…` immediately, 
increments `attempt`, and the same 120 ms effect then settles to `success` (the 
`?sync=error` query only produces the error state on the initial attempt, 
`attempt === 0`).\n- Error state now includes `<button type="button" 
className="secondary sync-retry">Retry sync</button>` inside the existing 
`role="alert"` error block. The error state (and hence the button) is only 
rendered when `state === \'error\'`, so it disappears after success and never 
appears on the success path.\n- No page reload, no global/URL mutation, and 
`AsyncPanel` owns only its own state, so the selected task in `App.tsx` is 
untouched.\n\n**`src/styles.css`** — added `.sync-retry { justify-self: start; 
margin-top: 8px; }` reusing the existing `button.secondary` styling.\n\n## 
Commands run\n- `npm run verify:agent` → `tsc --noEmit && vite build` succeeded;
`playwright test` → **6 passed** (including the existing "sync success state 
replaces the loading state").\n\n## Remaining risks\n- The `Retry sync` button 
sits inside the existing `role="alert"` region. Some screen readers may announce
the button text as part of the alert; I left the structure unchanged to preserve
the existing alert semantics and any existing container-scoped queries.\n- Retry
deliberately ignores `?sync=error` after the first attempt (always resolves to 
success) — intended by the spec, but it means the query string no longer 
reflects the displayed state.', 'aid': 0})
